ctor power come together would be  dominated by the climate agenda and  while it's featured in many of the  panels here in davos the war in ukraine  has undoubtedly  stolen some of the oxygen and certainly  the short-term attention of political  leaders but particularly the european  ones who are here but what we heard  there from john kerry and from ursula  london line is that they could hope to  perhaps achieve a virtuous circle from  the necessity of cutting reliance on  russian oil and gas as a means of  cutting off vladimir putin's means of  funding his war in ukraine that could  hasten perhaps european and other  countries towards ending their reliance  on those fossil fuels and investing in  renewables so trying to harness the  short-term crisis just a few thousand  kilometers to the east of here towards  the longer term goals on the horizon of  cutting greenhouse gases is clearly what  particularly john kerry was trying to  get across perhaps the most significant  climate signal of all however is the  fact and the man that john kerry was  sitting next to on the stage his chinese  counterpart sharing a platform  expressing chinese government's view  that it is fully committed to reducing  greenhouse gases in the long term there  has been very little dialogue between  the us and china at any level that's  this little signal of a rapprochement  can some closeness and alliance over  climate maybe perhaps the most that  climate lobby can take away from davos  this year  now the energy price cap is expected to  rise in october to around 2  800 pounds that's according to the uk's  energy regulator jonathan breely told  the business energy and industrial  strategy committee that the amount  people will need to pay for gas and  electricity will rise by about 40  percent  the cap is currently 1  971 pounds the business secretary quasi  quatern told the same committee that  he's not keen on the proposal of a  windfall tax on oil and gas producers as  a solution  i've been very clear about the windfall  tax i don't think uh it supports  investment i don't think uh it's  necessarily the right thing but as i  always say uh that's up to the chance  that he sees  the the economy across the piece  he is responsible for fiscal policy  and  he's instinctively against windfall  taxes but if he feels that these  extraordinary times require  extraordinary measures  that's up to him  well joining me now our reporter  madeline ratcliffe so it looks like a  windfall tax on energy companies isn't  going to be imminent so are there any  short-term solutions to bringing  people's energy bills down  yes actually i mean as you saw from that  grab there isn't a huge amount of  consensus coming from government at the  moment about how to deal with this  crisis i mean which is not looming it is  here for so many people and the current  um financial incentives and rebates  aren't helping what what hasn't been  discussed yet is insulation of course we  had so much about during the interlake  britain protests it's not just activists  that have called for it labour's shadow  um climate secretary ed miliband has  asked the government to look at um  insulation friends of the earth suggest  that better insulation could save 40 off  energy bills for 8 million households  this could be  most energy experts that agree a really  long-term sustainable way of saving  money for households and of course  meeting our climate goals because if  less energy is wasted escapes from the  house we need less um gas or electricity  to heat it and as a country we will we  will use less and just this afternoon  the energy and climate intelligence unit  double down on those calls for more  insulation as a means to meet this  problem so i'd expect him more calls for  insulation in the coming days  interesting stuff madeleine thanks very  much indeed  let's get more then of the day's climate  news now and the heat wave that's baked  parts of india and pakistan over the  past three months is likely to happen  more frequently in future according to  an international group of scientists  the world weather attribution group  found that the heat wave was made far  more likely due to climate change and  with current levels of global warming  deadly heat waves are 30 times more  likely to hit south asia  the dutch airline klm is being hauled up  in court over accusations that its  advertising is misleading and amounts to  greenwashing in what's believed to be a  world first case against the aviation  industry  environmental lawyers client earth will  argue that the airline's adverts and  carbon offsetting scheme give a false  illusion of the sustainability of its  flights and plans to tackle its  pollution  they claim this violates european  consumer law klm has so far not  commented  protesters have interrupted shell's  annual shareholder meeting which was  being held in the uk for the first time  activists from extinction rebellion and  other groups glued themselves to chairs  and interrupted proceedings reading out  speeches accusing shell of human rights  abuses it comes as the chief executive  of offshore energies uk deidre michie  told an energy conference that  environmental activists could undermine  the uk's energy security  and put net zero ambitions at risk this  she said is because a disruptive protest  damages investor confidence in the uk  and would potentially lead to dependence  on other countries for energy supplies  now carbon dioxide is the greenhouse gas  most responsible for warming the planet  most of it coming from burning fossil  fuels but the focus on cutting just  carbon dioxide is not enough to stop  global warming now new research points  out that a sharper focus on other  emissions such as methane would help to  reduce heating in the short term and  keep that 1.5 degree limit within reach  and if we look at our data dashboard now  the global warming index currently  estimates that we're above 1.2 degrees  already  now the other important chart is this  one so this we can see here is the  concentration of methane in the  atmosphere rising very fast as you can  see there methane is in the short term a  much more powerful pollutant the carbon  dioxide but it also leaves the  atmosphere much faster so any reduction  in emissions would have a much faster  positive effect well let's talk about  this a little bit more now because  joining me now is jonathan banks he's  the global director of the methane  program with the clean air task force so  welcome to you and what difference would  it make to climate change if we cut  methane emissions as well as carbon  dioxide  well the difference is quite dramatic  if we cut methane emissions  today  by roughly 45 percent  we can knock off 0.3 degrees celsius  from the warming that we're currently  feeling when you say 0.3 it doesn't  sound like much but  when you talk about climate change and  the 1.2 degrees that you're showing on  the previous chart 0.3 is a huge deal  methane is the only thing that we have  are the only tool that we have to start  slowing both the rate of warming as well  as total warming  in the near term if we do these kinds of  reductions we can see a dramatic impact  in the next 20 or 30 years which is huge  well yeah and encouraging if it can be  achieved so how do you go about it how  do you cut methane  well i think that's the other very  hopeful thing about  working on this issue is  these are things that don't require you  know  technologies that don't exist today  these are things that require best  practices  and good technologies that people are  already using in the oil and gas space  it's about good practices fixing leaks  replacing equipment that's designed to  leak gas  i always talk about this as it's not  rocket science it's plumbing and that's  basically what it is we have everything  we need right now to be able to address  these emissions and get the benefit of  the climate and um uh immediately so  we've got the tools there is there the  political will as well we did see that  deal didn't we i think it was that the  us and the eu launched this pledge to  cut methane emissions by 30 by 2030  before cop last year so does that mean  we're on track  it means we have the political will and  now we need the implementation of that  um  the global methane pledge  brings together over 110 countries  pledging to reduce their methane  emissions  but a pledge is not action and i think  that's the critical piece between now  and the next copt in in egypt we need to  see countries stepping forward to take  action to make good on the commitments  that they've made to the global meeting  pledge okay jonathan banks very  interesting to get your take on it  thanks very much indeed  thank you and that's it from me today  thanks for watching i'll hand you back  to sarah jane

